      "derivation": "A `receive` carrying one `cargo`, spent by an ordinary standing this door granted in advance — Article IX: \"an ordinary field spent by an ordinary standing granted in advance.\" The cargo names a class by digest and never carries its text: \"`receive` carries a digest, never the blueprint's text, and the digest identifies rather than delivers.\" This door already runs that class, so it may take the being in. **The answer is pinned, and it is the second rotation**: Article XIII calls migration \"a double rotation: to the committed heir, then immediately to a key the destination warden generated and the origin never saw\", and Article IX says which of the two keys a destination mints the commitment is of — \"the one the being is named by and that one's heir — and the commitment is of the first\", hashed under the destination's own name. So the answer is that hash and nothing else. What arrived is asserted whole: the replay record with the mark and the numbers already spent beneath it, the outbound row with both of its counters and both of the voice's keys — \"the voice's keys means both of them\" — and the standing's `name`, still the origin's, because \"every heir commitment was hashed under the name the door had then\" and a row that forgot it could never verify an older commitment again. **That field is IX-2 and this is the only place in the suite it can fail**: at every other door the name a commitment was minted under is that door's own name, so keeping it and re-deriving it are the same answer and no case could separate them. Here the arriving row's name is the origin house's and the door reading it is the destination — two different keys — so a door that stamped its own name on what arrived, which is the tidier-looking thing to do, would show its name here and be caught.",
