      "name": "the new house sends the second of the two news",
      "law": "The news",
      "obligations": ["XIV-13", "XIV-11", "XIV-12"],
      "derivation": "Article XIV: \"Migration is that message sent twice. First the origin's committed heir ... **Then the key the destination generated, sent by the new house itself.**\" **This is the second, and the departure scenario drives the first**; neither cites XIV-13 alone, because a case showing one message while citing an article about two would be claiming twice what it had shown once. Nothing crosses inward: the door composes what it owes the peer that arrived with the standings. **The word is a succession of the being to the key this door minted** — which is the key the peer can believe it from, because the commitment the first news carried was the hash of exactly this key, and Article XIV gives no other road: \"the successor signs and the peer hashes, and matching, the news is true.\" The being it names is the name the being wore at the house it left, because that is the name the peer still knows it by. **It carries the name, padlock and hints of this house** — Article XIV: \"Either may carry the new name and hints when where-it-answers has changed\", and for a migration it always has — so the peer rewrites its outbound row entire off the word's own fields. **The hints are this door's own roads and are not empty**, which is what makes XIV-12 assertable here: an empty list would have meant the road did not change, and for a being that has moved house that is the one thing that cannot be true. A door that sent the second news signed by its own name rather than the being's new key composes bytes no peer can believe, and misses this pin.",
